          { name: "Running Unopposed as a Strategic Vulnerability Rather Than a Win", tag: "avoid", why: "Reed won unopposed in 2024, which on its surface appears decisive but actually represents incomplete information about the district's political baseline. An unopposed result tells nothing about whether the seat is genuinely safe, whether opposition was prevented by redistricting or candidate recruitment rather than voter preference, or whether there is latent demand for an alternative. The high education level (49.3%), young median age (25.6), and renter majority (46.3%) in an unopposed race leave the actual coalition fragile and unmeasured—any future challenger who organizes around housing, education costs, or economic mobility could find more receptive ground than the unopposed margin suggests." },
          { name: "Assuming Educational and Income Levels Guarantee Party Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "The 49.3% college attainment rate in SD-7 is high, and at first glance college-educated voters appear reliably Republican in South Dakota. However, college-educated voters aged 25-35 (the likely concentration given the 25.6 median age) increasingly evaluate on economic outcomes—housing costs, student debt management, healthcare access—rather than inherited partisan identity. A legislator who treats this constituency as a locked-in Republican base rather than as swing voters evaluating on outcomes misses the demographic signal that this district is younger, more educated, and more economically fluid than traditional Republican strongholds, and runs the risk of finding a credible challenger who takes these material concerns seriously." }
