          { name: "Understanding the 48-Point Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 52% result in a South Dakota state senate seat is functionally a tied race and demands rigorous analysis of what generated the 48-point opposition before assuming it is confined to a specific constituency or driven by a single issue. Whether the opposition concentrates among renters frustrated by housing costs, among Native American voters mobilized around tribal concerns, among younger adults evaluating on economic mobility and wage growth, or distributed across multiple constituencies, the strategic response is fundamentally different. Without specific opposition research, any candidate risks misdiagnosing the core vulnerability and losing the seat in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Campaigning This Seat as Though 52% Represents a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a plurality of contested voters in a district where nearly half the electorate actively chose someone else. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though the margin is stable, rather than the product of specific consolidation around renter concerns and Native American engagement, will find that assumption punished when opposition candidates mobilize around those same issues more effectively. This is a seat that demands active constituent engagement and concrete policy answers, not the confidence of a landslide holder." },
          { name: "Treating Native American Voters as a Special Interest Rather Than a Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where a 12% demographic gap likely represents a meaningful Native American population and where the margin is only 52-48, positioning tribal governments or Native American voters as a side constituency rather than part of the core coalition is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability. Native American voters in South Dakota have demonstrated the capacity to mobilize decisively around state-tribal partnership issues when they perceive genuine engagement versus transactional outreach. Any candidate whose framing or record treats tribal governments as special interests competing with other constituents will find that framing actively hostile to the material reality and political capacity of a significant portion of the district." }
