          "The 23.6% college attainment rate, while lower than some districts, signals a constituency with meaningful access to education and professional employment — likely concentrated in regional healthcare, government, agriculture, and small business. This is not a district of poverty or systemic disadvantage, but it is also not a district of wealth or protected assets. The combination of homeownership concentration and moderate income means property tax policy, healthcare access and cost, and rural infrastructure quality are the issues most likely to activate electoral challenge. A representative who has not built relationships with local business leaders, healthcare providers, school districts, and local government — the connective tissue of a moderate-income rural or exurban community — has allowed those relationships to atrophy through six years of unopposed governance, creating precisely the conditions under which an organized challenger can gain traction."
